From 02c51f4867c40c512b9ec021ff5687dba3a7e769 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jernej Skrabec <jernej.skrabec@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 20 Jul 2026 23:32:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: sunxi: Add Allwinner DI300 deinterlace driver

Add a V4L2 mem2mem driver for the DI300 deinterlace core found on the
Allwinner H616 and later SoCs. It is modelled on the existing sun8i-di
driver but targets the newer register layout: the DI300 consumes up to
three temporally adjacent frames (previous, current and next) and, in
motion-adaptive mode, emits both progressive fields of the middle frame
in a single hardware pass. Bootstrap frames without full history fall
back to spatial (bob) interpolation.

Only the plain deinterlace (DIT) and motion-detect (MD) paths are used;
film-mode detection and temporal noise reduction are left unconfigured.

The deinterlace and motion-detect crop windows must be programmed to the
full frame on every run: they power up as an empty (zero) window, and
left at zero the engine latches busy but processes nothing and never
raises its completion interrupt.

device_run() picks the front two buffers of the capture ready queue
explicitly, and the IRQ handler completes those same two by pointer.
v4l2_m2m_next_dst_buf() / v4l2_m2m_last_dst_buf() only name the right
pair when exactly two capture buffers are queued; a client that
pre-queues more (ffmpeg's deinterlace_v4l2m2m filter queues six) would
otherwise get stale buffers completed instead of the ones hardware
wrote.

Motion mode needs only prev[0] rather than two full frames of history.
With prev[1] duplicating curr that pair simply reads as "no motion",
leaving the genuine next-vs-curr comparison to drive the blend, so
gating on both only delayed motion-adaptive output by a frame at stream
start.

Signed-off-by: Jernej Skrabec <jernej.skrabec@siol.net>
